{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And when a stranger sojourns.</b> It said this with an additional vav, intending thereby to impose upon Israel the judgment of the converts, so that they not treat the commandments lightly. Therefore it says <b>and when a stranger sojourns</b>, adding to the preceding matter — and just as the preceding matter applies to the children of Israel, so this too applies to Israel." "<b>And he shall perform, etc.</b> It is necessary to know why it wrote ״וְעָשָׂה״ with an additional vav at the beginning of the matter. Further, why was it necessary to repeat and say <b>so shall he perform</b>? And it might appear that it comes to exclude a ger toshav, and therefore it says <b>and when a stranger sojourns among you a ger</b> — and he shall be of those who perform the pesach, meaning a ger who observes the Torah; concerning him I command you, as the statute of the pesach etc., <b>so shall he perform</b>. But this interpretation is not sufficient, for it is self-understood that it speaks only of a full convert." "It may be explained according to the words of the Sages of blessed memory in Sifrei (Numbers 9:14), and this is their language: Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar says: If one converted between two pesachs, I would have thought he must perform the second one — Scripture therefore says ״וּלְאֶזְרַח״: just as an ezrach who did not perform [the first one is not obligated in the second] etc., end quote. And this is what the verse means by <b>and he shall perform</b> with an additional vav — to say: if he was included in the first pesach's obligation, then he is obligated to perform the second one when he was unable [to do the first]. According to this, its saying <b>and he shall perform</b> is a condition: only then is it <b>the statute of the pesach etc. he shall perform</b> — meaning, the statute of the pesach is one that exists also in the make-up offering; but if he was not in the first pesach, he is not in the second pesach." "<b>Further</b>, it appears to explain, raising yet another question: why did the verse specify the ger in this commandment more than in all the other commandments? And although the Sages of blessed memory said in Sifrei, and this is their language: I know only regarding pesach, that the ger is equated with the ezrach — from where do I derive all the commandments in the Torah? Scripture says ״חֻקָּה אַחַת״ etc., end quote — despite all this, why did it choose to specify only in the commandment of pesach? Indeed, because there is room to say that the ger is not obligated in pesach, since neither he nor his fathers were in Egypt, and there is no place for the act of pesach among them, for HaShem did not pass over their houses in Egypt. And this resembles what the Sages of blessed memory said (Bikkurim 1:4): a ger brings [first fruits] but does not recite, since he cannot say [״…that HaShem swore to our fathers…״] etc. — so too is this matter. Therefore the verse introduced this ruling here and said <b>and when a stranger sojourns among you a ger and he shall perform a pesach</b>, adding an explanatory addition: that even though he is not among those who came out of Egypt, nevertheless — aside from the fact that he must perform all the commandments — he must also do <b>this: and he shall perform a pesach</b> etc." "<b>And He was precise</b> to say <b>among you</b>, to say that even though you see this one as newly arrived from nearby, despite all this he is <b>among you</b> as one of you, and our exodus from Egypt was a world-redemption also for the soul of the ger — even though he was not present in the revealed [realm], he was present in the hidden [realm], for the root of holiness is one. And when our fathers were in Egypt, the totality of holiness was immersed in the impurity of Egypt, and had the totality of holiness — heaven forbid — been permanently immersed, there would be no remedy for the branches, for everything follows the root, and the deliverance of the root extends also to the branches." "And the verse says <b>כחקת הפסח</b> — the verse was concerned lest a person say: it is true that one must perform all the laws of the Pesach offering, but things that are utterly words of falsehood — such as saying, to recite ״עבדים היינו לפרעה במצרים ויוציאנו״ and so forth, and the like — I still maintain that they need not rely on falsehood. Therefore Scripture says ״כחקת הפסח״ — for one who speaks could conceivably say these things, since he too, in truth, was also in Egypt in the manner of the roots. And see what I explained on the verse (Exodus 23:9): ״ואתם ידעתם את נפש הגר״." "And His saying <b>כן יעשה</b> comes to establish an obligation — lest you say that the verse comes only as permission, that one may do so if he wishes; therefore Scripture says ״כן יעשה״ — as an obligation. And the statement <b>חקה אחת</b> comes to draw an equal law for the entire Torah:"], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {5 "The referent of 'a person' (האומר) and the precise logical flow of this comment are somewhat unclear; translated hyper-literally to preserve the syntax of the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:07:22.580529087Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}